1. Chosen Development Model
Model: Waterfall
Justification: Waterfall was chosen because the project requirements were clear from the start, the scope is fixed, and the DMAIC methodology follows the same sequential phase-by-phase structure.

2. User Requirements
a. Stakeholders

Bank Customers — book appointments online to avoid queuing
Bank Staff — manage the daily appointment queue
Admin (Bank Manager) — full control over the system
Development Team — build and maintain the system
Epoka University — evaluate the project academically

b. User Stories

As a customer, I want to book a bank appointment online so that I can avoid waiting in a queue.
As a customer, I want to choose a date and time slot so that I can plan my visit.
As a customer, I want to receive a confirmation code so that I can identify my appointment.
As a customer, I want to cancel my booking so that I can free up my slot if plans change.
As a customer, I want to use QueueBot so that I can get quick answers about services and hours.
As a staff member, I want to see today's appointments so that I can serve customers efficiently.
As a staff member, I want to mark appointments as Completed or No-show so that the system stays updated.
As an admin, I want to manage staff and services so that the system stays accurate and up to date.
As an admin, I want to view booking analytics so that I can monitor system performance.


3. Functional Requirements
a. Description

The system must allow customers to book an appointment by selecting a service, date, and time slot.
The system must generate a unique confirmation code for every booking.
The system must prevent double-booking of the same time slot.
The system must allow customers to view and cancel their bookings.
The system must block weekends and past dates from selection.
The system must allow staff to log in and manage appointments.
The system must allow staff to mark appointments as Completed or No-show.
The system must allow the admin to manage staff, services, and all bookings.
The system must include a QueueBot chatbot that answers common customer questions.

b. Acceptance Criteria
Appointment Booking

Customer selects a service, date, and available time slot
Booked slots are visually marked and cannot be selected
A unique QB-XXXXX code is generated after confirming
Booking appears immediately in My Bookings

My Bookings

Customer can search and view all their bookings
Customer can cancel a Confirmed booking

Staff Dashboard

Staff can log in with Employee ID and password
Staff can view, search, and filter all appointments
Staff can mark appointments as Completed or No-show with one click

Admin Panel

Admin logs in with separate credentials
Admin can manage bookings, staff, and services
Admin can view charts and export data


4. Non-Functional Requirements
a. Description

Usability: Easy to use for customers with no technical experience
Performance: Pages and actions must respond without delay
Reliability: No booking data should be lost
Accessibility: Must work on desktop, tablet, and mobile
Security: Staff and admin portals require login before access

b. Acceptance Criteria

Pages load in under 2 seconds
Booking process completes in under 5 seconds
Bookings persist after closing and reopening the browser
Website works correctly on Chrome, Safari, and Firefox
Staff and admin pages are only accessible after login


5. Application Specifications
a. Architecture
The system follows a three-tier client-side architecture.
The frontend consists of four HTML pages styled with CSS. 
The logic layer is handled by JavaScript files for each page. 
Data is stored in the browser's localStorage with no external database. 
A lightweight Node.js server handles QueueBot chat requests.

b. Database Model
Data is stored in browser localStorage as JSON. Three collections are used. 
Bookings store the confirmation code, service, date, time slot, customer details,
and status. Staff stores Employee ID, name, email, and role. Services store the 
service name, icon, description, and duration. Each booking code is unique and each 
time slot can only be booked once per date.

c. Technologies Used

HTML5 — page structure
CSS3 — styling and responsive design
JavaScript — interactivity and booking logic
Node.js and Express — QueueBot backend server
localStorage — data storage without a database
Google Fonts — Playfair Display and Nunito typography
SVG — custom illustrations

d. User Interface Design
The interface uses a pastel green color palette with sage green, mint, teal, and cream. The Home page features an illustrated bank scene with a teller, customers, and a waiting area. The User Portal has a four-step booking wizard. The Staff Dashboard shows KPI cards and an appointment table. The Admin Panel has a sidebar with sections for bookings, staff, services, and settings.
e. Security Measures

Role-based access: separate portals for Customer, Staff, and Admin
Staff login requires Employee ID and password
Admin login uses separate credentials
All booking forms validate required fields before saving
API credentials are stored only in the server file, never in the browser
